    "title": "Active Zone Minutes (AZM): Why Intensity Trumps Raw Step Counts",
    "description": "How the WHO-backed metric uses personalized heart rate thresholds to reward cardiovascular effort rather than simple movement volume.",
    "content": "### 1. Moving Beyond the 10,000-Step Myth\nFor decades, physical activity was measured solely through pedometer step counts. However, 10,000 slow, casual steps do not stimulate myocardial hypertrophy, increase capillary density, or improve mitochondrial volume in the same way structured cardiovascular effort does. **Active Zone Minutes (AZM)** changes the paradigm by quantifying cardiac work.\n\n### 2. How Active Zone Minutes are Calculated\nAZM uses your personalized resting heart rate and age to establish three dynamic training tiers:\n1. **Fat Burn Zone (Moderate Exertion: ~50%–69% HR Max):**\n   - *Multiplier:* **1 AZM** earned for every minute spent here.\n   - *Physiological Goal:* Aerobic base development and utilization of free fatty acids for fuel.\n2. **Cardio Zone (Vigorous Exertion: ~70%–84% HR Max):**\n   - *Multiplier:* **2 AZM** earned per minute.\n   - *Physiological Goal:* Expanding stroke volume, increasing VO2 Max, and improving glycogen storage capacity.\n3. **Peak Zone (Maximum Effort: 85%+ HR Max):**\n   - *Multiplier:* **2 AZM** earned per minute.\n   - *Physiological Goal:* Neuromuscular recruitment, lactic acid buffering, and anaerobic power.\n\n### 3. The 150-Minute Clinical Target\nMajor health authorities—including the American Heart Association (AHA) and World Health Organization (WHO)—recommend at least **150 minutes of moderate activity** or **75 minutes of vigorous activity** per week. AZM aligns directly with this guideline: by earning 150 AZMs weekly, you significantly lower cardiovascular disease incidence and all-cause mortality.",
