    "title": "Stress Management Score: How Wearable Sensors Quantify Autonomic Strain",
    "description": "How multi-sensor inputs—electrodermal activity (EDA), heart rate variability, and sleep architecture—combine to score your daily stress resilience.",
    "content": "### 1. What is the Stress Management Score?\nThe Stress Management Score is a daily evaluation ranging from 1 to 100 that quantifies your body's physiological resilience against psychological, metabolic, and physical stressors. Rather than asking how stressed you subjectively feel, it measures your autonomic nervous system's objective physiological state.\n\n### 2. The Three Algorithmic Sub-Scores\n1. **Autonomic Responsiveness (EDA & HRV - 40% Weight):**\n   - *Electrodermal Activity (EDA):* Sensors detect microscopic changes in sweat gland perspiration along the skin, which are directly innervated by the sympathetic nervous system. Rapid EDA fluctuations signal acute sympathetic arousal.\n   - *Nocturnal HRV:* High beat-to-beat variability reflects parasympathetic buffering capacity.\n2. **Exertion Balance (30% Weight):**\n   Evaluates whether your recent physical activity matches your long-term fitness baseline. Acute spikes in physical strain reduce your reserve capacity to handle mental stress.\n3. **Sleep Architecture (30% Weight):**\n   Sleep debt and disrupted sleep stages directly impair the prefrontal cortex's ability to inhibit amygdalar emotional reactivity.\n\n### 3. How to Action Your Score\n- **80–100 (High Resilience):** Your body is in autonomic equilibrium. Excellent day for handling demanding negotiations, tough cognitive tasks, or intense training.\n- **Below 60 (Autonomic Overload):** Your nervous system is operating on sympathetic overdrive. Schedule 10 minutes of breathwork, step outdoors, and reduce optional cognitive stressors.",
    "takeaway": "Your Stress Score reflects your physiological buffering capacity; a low score warns that your body is operating on sympathetic overdrive.",
